---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_URL } from '../consts';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'About', url: `${SITE_URL}/about/` },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About NSN Explorer: Free Government Parts Data"
  description="NSN Explorer is a free, independent index of U.S. government parts solicitations and prices built from public DIBBS, SAM.gov and USAspending records."
  pageType="AboutPage"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
  <h1>About {SITE_NAME}</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    NSN Explorer is an independent, free index of what the U.S. government buys in parts: the
    solicitations open for bid now, the prices paid before, and the companies that won. It is built
    for suppliers who want to find and win federal parts work.
  </p>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p class="gloss">
    The data is public, but it is scattered across systems built for procurement officers, not for
    the businesses that could fill the orders. NSN Explorer puts the same public facts in one
    place: what is open now, per part, over time, with a link back to the source record behind
    every figure, so a supplier can find work and quote it with confidence. The same index also
    serves buyers sizing up a category, researchers tracking federal demand and journalists
    checking a number.
  </p>

  <h2>What is on the site</h2>
  <ul class="gloss">
    <li><a href="/open/">Open demand</a>: parts solicitations open for bid today, by supply class, with quantity, return-by date and the last price paid.</li>
    <li><a href="/services/">Services</a>: open service contract notices from SAM.gov.</li>
    <li><a href="/catalog/">Catalog</a> and <a href="/browse/">price history</a>: every part with buying activity, its purchase prices, part numbers, characteristics and, for many parts, a dimensioned drawing.</li>
    <li><a href="/supplier/">Suppliers</a> and <a href="/agencies/">agencies</a>: who wins awards and who buys.</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/">Guides</a>: plain-language explanations of NSNs, CAGE codes, supply classes, competition codes and how to bid on DIBBS.</li>
    <li>Tools: <a href="/compare/">compare parts side by side</a>, <a href="/lookup/">look up a whole list of NSNs</a>, and a bid calculator on part and solicitation pages.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
  <p class="gloss">
    Solicitations and awards come from DIBBS, the Defense Logistics Agency's bid board. Contract
    notices come from SAM.gov and contract actions from USAspending. Part identification,
    characteristics and management data come from DLA's public PUB LOG and FLIS files, and company
    names and locations from the public CAGE file. Each source is checked every day and the site
    is rebuilt from it daily. The <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> page explains every
    figure and shows how fresh each source is.
  </p>
  <p class="gloss">
    Nothing on this site is estimated or modeled unless it is labelled as an estimate, and every
    figure links to the public record it came from. If you find an error,
    <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> and we will fix it.
  </p>

  <h2>What we are not</h2>
  <p class="gloss">
    We are not a government entity. We do not sell parts to the government through this site, and we
    do not broker or submit bids on anyone's behalf. Bids are placed on the government's own
    systems; our pages link to them.
  </p>

  <h2>Who runs it</h2>
  <p class="gloss">
    NSN Explorer is operated by NAO Parts LLC, a United States company. The site is free to use.
    How to reach us is on the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>; how we handle information is in
    the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> and the <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Accounts and our crawler</h2>
  <p class="gloss">
    Optional accounts sign in through <a href="https://workos.com" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">WorkOS</a>.
    Watchlists and saved searches are stored with our database provider; see the
    <a href="/privacy/#accounts">privacy policy</a> for what an account keeps.
  </p>

  <p class="gloss" id="crawler">
    <strong>NSNExplorerBot.</strong> For the supplier pages we read a few public pages of each company's own website
    (the home page and, where they exist, its about, capabilities, quality and contact pages) so a supplier's page can say what the
    company says it makes and how to reach it. The crawler identifies itself as
    <code>Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; NSNExplorerBot/1.0; +https://nsnexplorer.com/about/#crawler)</code> (or, if a site refuses that
    once, <code>NSNExplorerBot/1.0 (+https://nsnexplorer.com/about/#crawler)</code>), fetches and obeys the site's
    <code>robots.txt</code>, reads at most four pages per site, waits at least two seconds between requests to one site, and
    revisits a site about twice a year. If you run a website and do not want it read, disallow <code>NSNExplorerBot</code> in
    <code>robots.txt</code> or <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>email us</a>. Suppliers can also ask us to hide their contact
    details or website summary; see <a href="/privacy/#suppliers">Supplier information</a> in the privacy policy.
  </p>

  <p class="gloss">
    Contact: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
